---
import:
  - practice_domain
  - web_agent_contract
description: "Web app for the dental voice receptionist MVP (single practice, no auth, English only). Provides an admin UI for the practice administrator to configure services, staff, working hours, and greeting; a patient-facing call simulator that puts the patient's browser into a LiveKit room with the voice agent; and a read-only log of past calls and bookings."
---

***definitions***
- :WebApp: is the web application implemented by this module. :WebApp: serves two browser-facing surfaces (the admin UI used by :PracticeAdmin: and the patient-facing call simulator used by :Patient:) and the JSON HTTP APIs that the voice agent calls during a :Call:.
- :AdminSurface: is the browser-facing part of :WebApp: used by :PracticeAdmin: to manage :Practice:, :Service:, :Staff:, :WorkingHours:, :Appointment:, and :Call: information.
- :AdminDashboard: is the admin overview page of :AdminSurface:. It helps :PracticeAdmin: see setup status and navigate to the detailed admin pages.
- :DemoSeedData: is a set of realistic demo records created by :WebApp: for hackathon demonstrations. :DemoSeedData: is internally marked as demo-owned so it can be updated by later seed runs without overwriting non-demo user data.

***implementation reqs***

- :WebApp: is implemented in TypeScript on Node.js 20 LTS or newer. The project must enable TypeScript `strict` mode and treat type errors as build failures.
- :WebApp: uses Next.js 15 with the App Router for both browser-rendered pages and HTTP API routes. Page routes live under `src/app/` and API routes live under `src/app/api/`. The generated `package.json` must depend on stable npm-published React and React DOM versions compatible with Next.js 15, not unpublished React release-candidate package versions. The dev server runs via `npm run dev` and serves on port 3000.
- :WebApp: uses Tailwind CSS together with shadcn/ui for the admin and simulator UI. The renderer should generate the shadcn/ui setup files and reusable components under `src/components/ui/`, and should build forms, tables, navigation, badges, alerts, dialogs, cards, buttons, inputs, selects, textareas, skeleton/loading states, and empty states from shadcn/ui-style components rather than unstyled HTML elements. The visual style should be a clean professional healthcare admin interface with clear spacing, readable tables, obvious actions, and accessible focus and error states. The renderer must generate `src/lib/utils.ts` exporting a `cn` helper (combining `clsx` and `tailwind-merge`) — this file is imported by every shadcn/ui component and must always be present. The `package.json` must list `clsx`, `tailwind-merge`, and `tailwindcss-animate` as runtime dependencies alongside the other shadcn/ui peer packages.
- The `src/components/ui/select.tsx` component must be a full shadcn/ui Select implementation backed by `@radix-ui/react-select`. It must export `Select`, `SelectContent`, `SelectItem`, `SelectTrigger`, `SelectValue`, `SelectGroup`, `SelectLabel`, `SelectSeparator` as named exports — exactly the same API that shadcn/ui ships. Generating a thin wrapper around a native `<select>` HTML element is forbidden: it would make `SelectContent`, `SelectItem`, `SelectTrigger`, and `SelectValue` undefined, causing a runtime "Element type is invalid" crash wherever those are used. `@radix-ui/react-select` must be listed in `package.json` `dependencies`.
- :WebApp: must include a global stylesheet (`src/app/globals.css`) imported by `src/app/layout.tsx`. The stylesheet must open with the three Tailwind directives (`@tailwind base`, `@tailwind components`, `@tailwind utilities`) and define all shadcn/ui CSS custom properties in a `:root` block. The renderer must also generate `tailwind.config.ts` (or `tailwind.config.js`) at the project root, with `content` paths covering `./src/**/*.{ts,tsx}` so Tailwind scans all source files. It must also generate `postcss.config.js` at the project root exporting `tailwindcss` and `autoprefixer` plugins. Without all three files present and correctly wired, Tailwind utility classes will not render in the browser.
- :WebApp: admin UI should follow the visual and component guidance in the [admin UI reference](resources/admin_ui_reference.md).
- The generated `package.json` must include every runtime package imported by generated source files. If the generated code imports date/time helpers such as `date-fns` or `date-fns-tz`, those packages must be listed in `dependencies` so `npm install` followed by `npm run dev` works without manual package additions.
- React server components are used by default. Client components are opted into with the `"use client"` directive only when interactive state is required (forms, dial pad, in-call UI).
- Data is persisted in PostgreSQL 16 running as a service in a `docker-compose.yml` file at the project root of the generated :WebApp:. The database name is `voice_receptionist` and the service is reachable from :WebApp: at the connection string in environment variable `DATABASE_URL`. A `npm run db:up` script in `package.json` starts the database (`docker compose up -d`) and `npm run db:down` stops it.
- Prisma is the ORM. The Prisma schema lives at `prisma/schema.prisma` and contains exactly one model per persisted concept: `:Practice:`, `:Service:`, `:Staff:`, `:WorkingHours:`, `:Appointment:`, `:Call:`, and `:StaffTask:`. Each model's fields match the attributes declared in the definitions one-to-one. The `:Call:.transcript` field is stored as a JSON column (`Json` in Prisma). The `:Practice:.hoursOfOperation` field is also a JSON column. All other fields use their natural relational types (string, integer, datetime). Every concept that has a functional spec for a backing API route must have a corresponding Prisma model — the schema and the API specs must stay in sync.
- Repository queries for :Call: rows must select only fields that exist on the Prisma `Call` model. `GET /api/calls` must not select or return a `createdAt` field because :Call: uses `startedAt` as its creation/start timestamp. The :Call: model has no `outcome` field — do not reference `outcome` anywhere in call-related repository or seed code.
- When creating or updating Prisma records that have relation fields (e.g. `Call.appointment`, `Appointment.staff`, `Appointment.service`), use the nested relation syntax (`{ connect: { id } }`) in `prisma.X.create()` calls. Never pass a bare foreign-key field name (e.g. `appointmentId`) directly inside a `prisma.call.create({ data: { ... } })` call — Prisma's checked input types require the relation object. For `prisma.X.update()`, the `UncheckedUpdateInput` type allows the scalar FK field directly and is acceptable.
- Database schema is managed by Prisma. On `npm run dev` startup, :WebApp: runs `prisma db push` (not `prisma migrate deploy`) to create or synchronise all tables directly from `prisma/schema.prisma` without requiring pre-existing migration files. This ensures tables are created correctly on the very first run of a freshly rendered project. The `npm run dev` script in `package.json` must be `npm run db:up && prisma db push && next dev`.
- :WebApp: integrates with LiveKit using the `livekit-server-sdk` npm package. This is used only on the server side for minting access tokens. The browser uses `@livekit/components-react` and `livekit-client` for joining rooms, capturing the microphone, and playing remote audio. LiveKit credentials (`LIVEKIT_URL`, `LIVEKIT_API_KEY`, `LIVEKIT_API_SECRET`) are read from server-side environment variables and are never exposed to the browser bundle.
- All inbound JSON request bodies are validated with `zod` schemas before reaching business logic. Validation failures produce the HTTP 400 responses described in the functional specs.
- Source code is organized under `src/` with the following layered structure: `src/app/` (pages and API route handlers — thin glue only), `src/components/` (React UI components), `src/services/` (business logic such as availability calculation, transcript appending, token minting — pure functions where possible), `src/repositories/` (Prisma-backed data access; the only layer that touches Prisma directly), `src/lib/` (cross-cutting helpers such as the Prisma client singleton, the LiveKit token helper, the zod schemas, and date/time utilities). API route handlers call services; services call repositories; UI components call API routes via `fetch`. Every entity that has a UI page must also have its complete backend generated in the same render: the Prisma model in `schema.prisma`, a repository file under `src/repositories/`, and the API route handler(s) under `src/app/api/`. Generating only the UI without the backing API causes 404 errors at runtime. This is a hard requirement — do not generate a page that calls an API endpoint unless that endpoint's route file is also generated.
- Date and time values in API payloads use the formats stated in the functional specs ("HH:MM" 24-hour for clock times, "YYYY-MM-DD" for dates, "YYYY-MM-DDTHH:MM" for local datetimes, ISO 8601 for server timestamps). All server-side timestamps are computed in UTC and serialised as ISO 8601 with offset `Z`. All local datetimes ("YYYY-MM-DDTHH:MM") are interpreted as the practice's local clock; the MVP assumes a single fixed timezone read from environment variable `PRACTICE_TIMEZONE` (default `America/New_York`).
- Local development configuration lives in `.env.local` (gitignored). Required env vars are documented in a committed `.env.example` file: `DATABASE_URL`, `LIVEKIT_URL`, `LIVEKIT_API_KEY`, `LIVEKIT_API_SECRET`, `PRACTICE_TIMEZONE`.
- Code style is enforced with ESLint and Prettier using the Next.js recommended configurations. CI-time formatting and lint must pass with no warnings.
- Admin table components that combine display, inline-editing, and delete-confirmation state must delegate per-row interactive logic to a dedicated row-level client component (e.g. `StaffTableRow`, `ServiceTableRow`). The parent table component renders the row list and passes mutation callbacks; the row component owns its own edit/delete UI state. This keeps each component function short and avoids JSX nesting errors from oversized render functions.
- Every page component's `return (...)` block must use exactly one root JSX element, and the opening and closing tags of that root element must match (e.g. if the return opens with `<main>`, it must close with `</main>` — never `</div>` or any other tag). Pages that contain multiple sections (form + table, header + card + list) must extract each section into a named sub-component so that no single component function exceeds roughly 80 lines. Mismatched root tags produce a compile-time "Unexpected token" syntax error that prevents the entire route from loading.
- Icons must be imported exclusively from `lucide-react`. The renderer must never define a local fallback icon component (e.g. a hand-written SVG function) with the same name as an imported lucide icon in the same file — doing so causes a duplicate-identifier compile error. If a lucide icon is imported at the top of a file, no local definition of that name may appear anywhere else in the same file.
- :WebApp: should include :UnitTests: covering services and repositories at minimum.

***test reqs***

- :UnitTests: of :WebApp: are written in TypeScript and run with Vitest.
- :UnitTests: are co-located next to the source files they cover, with the suffix `.test.ts` or `.test.tsx`. Vitest discovers them automatically under `src/`.
- :UnitTests: cover at minimum every module under `src/services/`, every module under `src/repositories/`, and every module under `src/lib/`. UI components are not required to have :UnitTests: for the MVP.
- :UnitTests: that exercise the repository layer run against a real PostgreSQL database (the same docker-compose Postgres service, but using a dedicated `voice_receptionist_test` database). Each repository test truncates the tables it touches in a `beforeEach` hook. Mocking the database client is forbidden in repository tests.
- :UnitTests: that exercise services and lib helpers may mock the repository layer using vitest's built-in mocking. Mocking the repository layer is required (not optional) when the service test is not specifically testing the data path.
- API-route tests (under `src/app/api/`) run as integration tests: they call the route handler with a constructed `Request` object, hit the real Postgres test database via the real repositories, and assert on the returned `Response`.
- :UnitTests: are executed by the script `test_scripts/run_unittests_typescript.sh`, which receives the rendered web project's folder as its first positional argument and is fully self-contained — every invocation stages the project into its own working folder, installs npm dependencies with `npm ci`, starts the docker-compose Postgres service in detached mode, waits for it to accept connections, creates the `voice_receptionist_test` database if it does not exist, runs `prisma generate` and `prisma migrate deploy` against the test `DATABASE_URL`, then invokes `vitest run` with coverage reporters disabled by default. The script must be safe to run multiple times back-to-back without state leaking between runs.

***functional specs***
- Implement the entry point for :WebApp:. :WebApp: listens on HTTP port 3000 on the local host. A request to the root path "/" returns an HTTP 200 response with a landing page that displays the product name "Voice Receptionist" and two prominent navigation buttons: "Call Simulator" linking to `/simulator` and "Admin Dashboard" linking to `/admin`. A request to the health-check endpoint "GET /api/health" returns an HTTP 200 response with JSON body `{"status":"ok"}`.
- Implement the practice-info HTTP API on :WebApp:.
  - `GET /api/practice` returns HTTP 200 with the JSON representation of the single :Practice: row, or HTTP 200 with body `{}` if no row exists yet.
  - `PUT /api/practice` accepts a JSON body and upserts the single :Practice: row.
  - The JSON representation has fields: `name` (string), `address` (string), `phoneDisplay` (string), `hoursOfOperation` (array of objects each with `dayOfWeek` of "Monday".."Sunday", `openTime` "HH:MM", `closeTime` "HH:MM"), `greetingStyle` (string, one of "warm" | "professional" | "friendly_first_name" | "custom"; default "warm"), `customGreeting` (string, optional), `toneProfile` (string, one of "friendly" | "professional" | "gentle"; default "friendly"), `closingStyle` (string, one of "standard" | "appointment_focused" | "custom"; default "standard"), `customClosing` (string, optional), `agentGender` (string, one of "female" | "male"; default "female"), `agentName` (string, optional).
  - PUT returns HTTP 200 with the saved JSON on success.
  - PUT returns HTTP 400 with body `{"error": "<message>"}` if `name`, `address`, or `phoneDisplay` is missing or empty, if any time string is not a valid "HH:MM" 24-hour value, or if `greetingStyle`, `toneProfile`, `closingStyle`, or `agentGender` is set to a value outside its allowed set.
  - The :Practice: row is persisted in the data store with one column per attribute, and survives :WebApp: restarts.

  ***acceptance tests***
  - After a valid `PUT /api/practice`, a later `GET /api/practice` after a :WebApp: restart should return the saved practice JSON.
  - A `PUT /api/practice` request with an empty `name` should return HTTP 400 and should not overwrite the previously saved practice row.
- Add the admin practice page to :WebApp: at the route `GET /admin/practice`.
  - The page is an HTML page that lets :PracticeAdmin: view the single :Practice: row in an editable form. No submit action is wired yet by this spec.
  - On page load the page calls `GET /api/practice` and pre-fills its form fields with the returned values; if the response is `{}` all fields start empty.
  - The form has labelled text inputs for `name`, `address`, and `phoneDisplay`, a repeatable list of HoursOfOperation rows where each row has a day-of-week dropdown (Monday..Sunday), an `openTime` input, and a `closeTime` input, an "Agent Voice" section, and an "Agent Personality" section.
  - Above the HoursOfOperation list there is an "Add day" button that appends a new empty row; each row has a "Remove" button that deletes that row.
  - The "Agent Voice" section contains: a labelled dropdown for `agentGender` with options "Female" (value "female", default) and "Male" (value "male"); and a labelled text input for `agentName` (placeholder "e.g. Alex, Maya") with a hint "If set, the agent introduces itself by this name at the start of each call." The `agentName` field is optional.
  - The "Agent Personality" section contains: a labelled dropdown for `greetingStyle` with options "Warm" (value "warm", default), "Professional" (value "professional"), "Friendly" (value "friendly_first_name"), "Custom" (value "custom"); a labelled text input for `customGreeting` that is only shown when `greetingStyle` is "custom"; a labelled dropdown for `toneProfile` with options "Friendly" (value "friendly", default), "Professional" (value "professional"), "Gentle" (value "gentle"); a labelled dropdown for `closingStyle` with options "Standard" (value "standard", default), "Appointment-focused" (value "appointment_focused"), "Custom" (value "custom"); and a labelled text input for `customClosing` that is only shown when `closingStyle` is "custom".
- Wire up saving on the admin practice page at `GET /admin/practice`.
  - Add a "Save" button below the form.
  - Clicking "Save" sends `PUT /api/practice` with the current form values as a JSON body matching the shape used by `GET /api/practice`.
  - On HTTP 200 from PUT the page shows a "Saved" confirmation banner above the form for at least 3 seconds before auto-dismissing.
  - On HTTP 400 from PUT the page shows the returned `error` message in a red banner above the form and keeps the user's current input.

  ***acceptance tests***
  - When saving succeeds, the admin practice page should show the "Saved" banner without clearing the saved form values.
  - When saving returns HTTP 400, the admin practice page should display the returned error message and preserve the unsaved form values.
- Implement the services list and create HTTP API on :WebApp:.
  - `GET /api/services` returns HTTP 200 with a JSON array of all :Service: rows ordered by name ascending. Each row has fields `id` (string), `name` (string), `durationMinutes` (integer), `priceCents` (integer), `description` (string), `category` (string, may be empty), `preProcedureInstructions` (string, may be empty), `postProcedureInstructions` (string, may be empty).
  - `POST /api/services` accepts a JSON body with `name`, `durationMinutes`, `priceCents`, `description` (all required), and optional `category`, `preProcedureInstructions`, `postProcedureInstructions` (all strings, default to empty string if omitted). Creates a new :Service: row. Returns HTTP 201 with the created :Service:'s JSON including its server-assigned `id`.
  - POST returns HTTP 400 with body `{"error": "<message>"}` if `name` is empty, `description` is empty, `durationMinutes` is not a positive integer multiple of 15, or `priceCents` is negative.
  - :Service: rows are persisted in the data store and survive :WebApp: restarts.

  ***acceptance tests***
  - Creating two valid :Service: rows with names "Whitening" and "Cleaning" should make `GET /api/services` return them ordered as "Cleaning" then "Whitening".
  - A `POST /api/services` request with `durationMinutes` equal to 10 should return HTTP 400 and should not create a :Service: row.
- Implement the services update and delete HTTP API on :WebApp:.
  - `PUT /api/services/:id` accepts a JSON body with the same shape as `POST /api/services` (including the optional fields) and replaces the matching :Service: row's mutable attributes. Returns HTTP 200 with the updated JSON.
  - PUT returns HTTP 404 with body `{"error": "not found"}` if no :Service: with that `id` exists.
  - PUT returns HTTP 400 with body `{"error": "<message>"}` on the same validation failures as POST.
  - `DELETE /api/services/:id` deletes the matching :Service: row. Returns HTTP 204 with empty body on success.
  - DELETE returns HTTP 404 with body `{"error": "not found"}` if no :Service: with that `id` exists.
- Add the admin services page to :WebApp: at the route `GET /admin/services`.
  - On page load the page calls `GET /api/services` and renders the returned :Service: rows as a table with columns Category, Name, Duration (in minutes), Price (formatted as US dollars), Description. The Category cell is empty if the service has no category set.
  - Below the table the page shows an "Add service" form with: text inputs for `name`, `durationMinutes`, `priceCents`, and `description` (all required); and optional inputs for `category` (text input, placeholder "e.g. Preventive, Cosmetic"), `preProcedureInstructions` (textarea, label "Pre-procedure instructions"), and `postProcedureInstructions` (textarea, label "Post-procedure instructions"). A "Create" button submits the form.
  - Clicking "Create" sends `POST /api/services` with the form values. On HTTP 201 the form is cleared and the page re-fetches and re-renders the table. On HTTP 400 the page shows the returned `error` message in a red banner above the form and keeps the user's current input.
- Wire up edit and delete on the admin services page at `GET /admin/services`.
  - Each table row has an "Edit" button and a "Delete" button.
  - Clicking "Edit" replaces that row in place with inputs pre-filled with the row's current values (including `category`, `preProcedureInstructions`, `postProcedureInstructions`), a "Save" button, and a "Cancel" button. Clicking "Save" sends `PUT /api/services/:id` and on HTTP 200 collapses the row back to display mode showing the new values. On HTTP 400 the inline edit shows the returned `error` message and keeps the user's current input. Clicking "Cancel" collapses the row back to display mode showing the original values.
  - Clicking "Delete" shows an inline confirmation prompt with "Confirm" and "Cancel" buttons. Clicking "Confirm" sends `DELETE /api/services/:id` and on HTTP 204 removes the row from the table.

  ***acceptance tests***
  - When an inline service edit returns HTTP 400, the row should remain in edit mode and show the returned error message.
  - After confirming deletion of a service that returns HTTP 204, that service should no longer appear in the services table.
- Implement the staff list and create HTTP API on :WebApp:.
  - `GET /api/staff` returns HTTP 200 with a JSON array of all :Staff: rows ordered by name ascending. Each row has fields `id` (string), `name` (string), `role` (string), `bio` (string, may be empty), `qualifications` (string, may be empty), `specialization` (string, may be empty).
  - `POST /api/staff` accepts a JSON body with `name`, `role` (both required, non-empty), `bio` (optional, defaults to empty string), `qualifications` (optional, defaults to empty string), and `specialization` (optional, defaults to empty string) and creates a new :Staff: row. Returns HTTP 201 with the created :Staff:'s JSON including its server-assigned `id`.
  - POST returns HTTP 400 with body `{"error": "<message>"}` if `name` or `role` is missing or empty.
  - :Staff: rows are persisted in the data store and survive :WebApp: restarts.

  ***acceptance tests***
  - Creating two valid :Staff: rows should make `GET /api/staff` return them ordered by name ascending.
  - A `POST /api/staff` request with an empty `role` should return HTTP 400 and should not create a :Staff: row.
- Implement the staff update and delete HTTP API on :WebApp:.
  - `PUT /api/staff/:id` accepts a JSON body with the same shape as `POST /api/staff` (including the optional `qualifications` and `specialization` fields) and replaces the matching :Staff: row's mutable attributes. Returns HTTP 200 with the updated JSON.
  - PUT returns HTTP 404 with body `{"error": "not found"}` if no :Staff: with that `id` exists.
  - PUT returns HTTP 400 with body `{"error": "<message>"}` on the same validation failures as POST.
  - `DELETE /api/staff/:id` deletes the matching :Staff: row and all of its :WorkingHours: entries. Returns HTTP 204 with empty body on success.
  - DELETE returns HTTP 404 with body `{"error": "not found"}` if no :Staff: with that `id` exists.
  - DELETE returns HTTP 409 with body `{"error": "staff has appointments"}` if any :Appointment: row exists whose StaffRef points at this :Staff: and whose Status is "booked".
- Add the admin staff page to :WebApp: at the route `GET /admin/staff`.
  - On page load the page calls `GET /api/staff` and renders the returned :Staff: rows as a table with columns Name, Role, Specialization (empty if unset), Qualifications (empty if unset), Bio.
  - Below the table the page shows an "Add staff" form with text inputs for `name`, `role`, `qualifications` (label "Qualifications", placeholder "e.g. DDS, DMD, RDH"), and `specialization` (label "Specialization", placeholder "e.g. General Dentistry, Cosmetic Dentistry"), and a textarea for `bio`, plus a "Create" button.
  - Clicking "Create" sends `POST /api/staff` with the form values. On HTTP 201 the form is cleared and the page re-fetches and re-renders the table. On HTTP 400 the page shows the returned `error` message in a red banner above the form and keeps the user's current input.
- Wire up edit and delete on the admin staff page at `GET /admin/staff`.
  - Each table row has an "Edit" button and a "Delete" button.
  - Clicking "Edit" replaces that row in place with text inputs pre-filled with the row's current values (including `qualifications` and `specialization`), a "Save" button, and a "Cancel" button. Clicking "Save" sends `PUT /api/staff/:id` and on HTTP 200 collapses the row back to display mode showing the new values. On HTTP 400 the inline edit shows the returned `error` message and keeps the user's current input. Clicking "Cancel" collapses the row back to display mode showing the original values.
  - Clicking "Delete" shows an inline confirmation prompt with "Confirm" and "Cancel" buttons. Clicking "Confirm" sends `DELETE /api/staff/:id` and on HTTP 204 removes the row from the table. On HTTP 409 the page shows the returned `error` message in a red banner near the row and leaves the row in place.

  ***acceptance tests***
  - When deleting a staff row returns HTTP 409 with `{"error": "staff has appointments"}`, the row should remain visible and the page should show that error near the row.
  - Clicking "Cancel" during staff inline editing should restore the row's original displayed values.
- Implement the working-hours list and create HTTP API on :WebApp:.
  - `GET /api/staff/:staffId/working-hours` returns HTTP 200 with a JSON array of all :WorkingHours: rows whose StaffRef matches `:staffId`, ordered by DayOfWeek then StartTime ascending. Each row has fields `id` (string), `staffId` (string), `dayOfWeek` ("Monday".."Sunday"), `startTime` ("HH:MM"), `endTime` ("HH:MM").
  - GET returns HTTP 404 with body `{"error": "staff not found"}` if no :Staff: with that `staffId` exists.
  - `POST /api/staff/:staffId/working-hours` accepts a JSON body with `dayOfWeek`, `startTime`, `endTime` (all required) and creates a new :WorkingHours: row linked to that staff member. Returns HTTP 201 with the created row's JSON including its server-assigned `id`.
  - POST returns HTTP 400 with body `{"error": "<message>"}` if any field is missing, if `dayOfWeek` is not one of "Monday".."Sunday", if either time is not a valid "HH:MM" 24-hour string aligned to a 15-minute mark (00, 15, 30, 45 minutes), or if `endTime` is not strictly later than `startTime`.
  - POST returns HTTP 404 if no :Staff: with that `staffId` exists.
  - :WorkingHours: rows are persisted in the data store and survive :WebApp: restarts.

  ***acceptance tests***
  - A `POST /api/staff/:staffId/working-hours` request with `startTime` "09:10" should return HTTP 400 and should not create a :WorkingHours: row.
  - After creating a valid :WorkingHours: row, a later `GET /api/staff/:staffId/working-hours` after a :WebApp: restart should include that row.
- Implement the working-hours update and delete HTTP API on :WebApp:.
  - `PUT /api/working-hours/:id` accepts a JSON body with the same shape as `POST /api/staff/:staffId/working-hours` (without `staffId`) and replaces the matching :WorkingHours: row's mutable attributes. Returns HTTP 200 with the updated JSON including its `staffId`.
  - PUT returns HTTP 404 with body `{"error": "not found"}` if no :WorkingHours: with that `id` exists.
  - PUT returns HTTP 400 with body `{"error": "<message>"}` on the same validation failures as POST.
  - `DELETE /api/working-hours/:id` deletes the matching :WorkingHours: row. Returns HTTP 204 with empty body on success.
  - DELETE returns HTTP 404 with body `{"error": "not found"}` if no :WorkingHours: with that `id` exists.
- Add the admin working-hours page to :WebApp: at the route `GET /admin/staff/:staffId/hours`.
  - On page load the page calls `GET /api/staff` and shows the matching :Staff: member's Name and Role at the top; if no :Staff: with that `staffId` exists the page renders a 404 message.
  - The page calls `GET /api/staff/:staffId/working-hours` and renders the returned :WorkingHours: rows as a table with columns Day, Start, End, ordered by day and start time.
  - Each row in `/admin/staff` (the staff table) has an additional "Edit hours" link that navigates to `/admin/staff/:id/hours`.
  - Below the working-hours table the page shows an "Add hours" form with a day-of-week dropdown (Monday..Sunday) and `startTime` / `endTime` inputs accepting "HH:MM" 24-hour values aligned to a 15-minute mark, plus a "Create" button.
  - Clicking "Create" sends `POST /api/staff/:staffId/working-hours` with the form values. On HTTP 201 the form is cleared and the page re-fetches and re-renders the working-hours table. On HTTP 400 the page shows the returned `error` message in a red banner above the form and keeps the user's current input.
- Wire up edit and delete on the admin working-hours page at `GET /admin/staff/:staffId/hours`.
  - Each working-hours row has an "Edit" button and a "Delete" button.
  - Clicking "Edit" replaces that row in place with a day dropdown and time inputs pre-filled with the row's current values, a "Save" button, and a "Cancel" button. Clicking "Save" sends `PUT /api/working-hours/:id` and on HTTP 200 collapses the row back to display mode showing the new values. On HTTP 400 the inline edit shows the returned `error` message and keeps the user's current input. Clicking "Cancel" collapses the row back to display mode showing the original values.
  - Clicking "Delete" shows an inline confirmation prompt with "Confirm" and "Cancel" buttons. Clicking "Confirm" sends `DELETE /api/working-hours/:id` and on HTTP 204 removes the row from the table.

  ***acceptance tests***
  - When a working-hours inline edit returns HTTP 400, the row should remain in edit mode and show the returned error message.
  - After confirming deletion of a working-hours row that returns HTTP 204, that row should no longer appear in the working-hours table.
- Implement the availability query HTTP API on :WebApp:.
  - `GET /api/availability?serviceId=<id>&from=<YYYY-MM-DD>&to=<YYYY-MM-DD>` returns HTTP 200 with a JSON array of available time slots.
  - Each element in the array is an object with `staffId` (string), `staffName` (string), `serviceId` (string), `startAt` (ISO 8601 local datetime "YYYY-MM-DDTHH:MM"), `endAt` (ISO 8601 local datetime "YYYY-MM-DDTHH:MM").
  - The list is computed by: looking up the :Service: with the given `serviceId`; for each :Staff: member, iterating each day from `from` to `to` inclusive; for each :WorkingHours: entry of that :Staff: whose DayOfWeek matches that date's day of the week, enumerating candidate start times every 15 minutes from `StartTime` up to `EndTime` minus the service's `DurationMinutes`; including a candidate only if no existing :Appointment: of the same :Staff: whose Status is "booked" overlaps the candidate window `[startAt, startAt + DurationMinutes)`.
  - The array is sorted by `startAt` ascending, with `staffId` ascending as a tiebreaker.
  - GET returns HTTP 400 with body `{"error": "<message>"}` if `serviceId` is missing or empty, if `from` or `to` is missing or not a valid `YYYY-MM-DD` date, or if `from` is strictly after `to`.
  - GET returns HTTP 404 with body `{"error": "service not found"}` if no :Service: with that `serviceId` exists.
- Implement the booking creation HTTP API on :WebApp:.
  - `POST /api/appointments` accepts a JSON body with `serviceId` (string), `staffId` (string), `patientPhone` (string), `patientName` (string), `startAt` (ISO 8601 local datetime "YYYY-MM-DDTHH:MM").
  - On success returns HTTP 201 with the created :Appointment: JSON including `id`, `serviceId`, `staffId`, `patientPhone`, `patientName`, `startAt`, `endAt` (computed as `startAt` plus the service's `DurationMinutes`), `status` ("booked"), `createdAt` (ISO 8601 timestamp).
  - POST returns HTTP 400 with body `{"error": "<message>"}` if any field is missing or empty, or `startAt` is not aligned to a quarter-hour mark (minute 00, 15, 30, or 45).
  - POST returns HTTP 404 with body `{"error": "service not found"}` if no :Service: matches `serviceId`, or `{"error": "staff not found"}` if no :Staff: matches `staffId`.
  - POST returns HTTP 409 with body `{"error": "slot unavailable"}` if the proposed `[startAt, endAt)` window is not contained within at least one :WorkingHours: entry of the given :Staff: on that day of week, or if it overlaps any existing :Appointment: of the same :Staff: whose Status is "booked".
  - The :Appointment: row is persisted in the data store and survives :WebApp: restarts.
- Implement the call create and transcript append HTTP API on :WebApp:.
  - `POST /api/calls` accepts a JSON body with `patientPhone` (string, required, non-empty) and creates a new :Call: row with `status` "in_progress", `startedAt` set to the current server timestamp in ISO 8601, an empty `transcript`, no `endedAt`, no `summary`, and no `appointmentId`. Returns HTTP 201 with the created :Call:'s JSON including its server-assigned `id`.
  - POST `/api/calls` returns HTTP 400 with body `{"error": "<message>"}` if `patientPhone` is missing or empty.
  - `POST /api/calls/:id/transcript` accepts a JSON body with `speaker` (one of "patient" or "agent") and `text` (string, non-empty) and appends one turn to the :Call:'s `transcript`. The appended turn has fields `speaker`, `text`, and a server-assigned `at` (ISO 8601 timestamp). Returns HTTP 200 with the updated :Call:'s JSON.
  - POST `/api/calls/:id/transcript` returns HTTP 400 with body `{"error": "<message>"}` if `speaker` is not "patient" or "agent", or if `text` is missing or empty.
  - POST `/api/calls/:id/transcript` returns HTTP 404 with body `{"error": "not found"}` if no :Call: with that `id` exists, and HTTP 409 with body `{"error": "call ended"}` if the call's `status` is "ended".
  - :Call: rows are persisted in the data store and survive :WebApp: restarts.
- Implement the call finalize HTTP API on :WebApp:.
  - `POST /api/calls/:id/end` accepts a JSON body with `summary` (string, may be empty), `appointmentId` (string, optional), and `outcome` (string, one of "resolved" | "needs_callback" | "needs_action"; optional; defaults to "resolved" if omitted). The endpoint sets the :Call:'s `status` to "ended", `endedAt` to the current server timestamp in ISO 8601, `summary` to the provided value, `outcome` to the provided value (or "resolved" if omitted), and `appointmentId` to the matching :Appointment:'s id if one was provided. Returns HTTP 200 with the updated :Call:'s JSON including `outcome`.
  - POST `/api/calls/:id/end` returns HTTP 400 with body `{"error": "<message>"}` if `outcome` is provided but is not one of "resolved", "needs_callback", or "needs_action".
  - POST `/api/calls/:id/end` returns HTTP 404 with body `{"error": "not found"}` if no :Call: with that `id` exists, or HTTP 404 with body `{"error": "appointment not found"}` if `appointmentId` was provided but no :Appointment: with that id exists.
  - POST `/api/calls/:id/end` returns HTTP 409 with body `{"error": "already ended"}` if the call's `status` is already "ended". The :Call: row is not modified in this case.
- Implement the staff task create HTTP API on :WebApp:.
  - `POST /api/tasks` accepts a JSON body with `callId` (string, required), `description` (string, required, non-empty), `type` (string, one of "callback" | "action"; required), `priority` (string, one of "normal" | "high"; optional; defaults to "normal"), `patientName` (string, optional; defaults to empty string), and `topic` (string, optional; defaults to empty string).
  - Creates a new :StaffTask: row with `status` "open", `createdAt` set to the current server timestamp in ISO 8601, and `patientPhone` copied from the referenced :Call:.
  - Returns HTTP 201 with the created :StaffTask:'s JSON including `id`, `callId`, `patientPhone`, `patientName`, `description`, `topic`, `type`, `priority`, `status`, `createdAt`, `resolvedAt` (null).
  - Returns HTTP 400 with body `{"error": "<message>"}` if `callId` is missing, `description` is empty, or `type` is not "callback" or "action".
  - Returns HTTP 404 with body `{"error": "call not found"}` if no :Call: with that `callId` exists.
  - :StaffTask: rows are persisted in the data store and survive :WebApp: restarts.
- Implement the staff task list HTTP API on :WebApp:.
  - `GET /api/tasks` accepts an optional query parameter `status` (one of "open" | "done" | "all"; defaults to "open").
  - Returns HTTP 200 with a JSON array of :StaffTask: rows matching the filter, ordered by `priority` descending ("high" before "normal") then `createdAt` descending.
  - Each element has fields: `id`, `callId`, `patientPhone`, `patientName`, `description`, `topic` (string, may be empty), `type`, `priority`, `status`, `createdAt`, `resolvedAt` (nullable).
  - Returns HTTP 400 with body `{"error": "<message>"}` if `status` is provided but is not one of "open", "done", or "all".
- Implement the staff task update HTTP API on :WebApp:.
  - `PUT /api/tasks/:id` accepts a JSON body with `status` (string, one of "open" | "done"; required).
  - When setting `status` to "done", sets `resolvedAt` to the current server timestamp in ISO 8601.
  - When setting `status` back to "open", clears `resolvedAt` (sets it to null).
  - Returns HTTP 200 with the updated :StaffTask:'s JSON including `topic`.
  - Returns HTTP 400 with body `{"error": "<message>"}` if `status` is not "open" or "done".
  - Returns HTTP 404 with body `{"error": "not found"}` if no :StaffTask: with that `id` exists.
- Implement the LiveKit access-token HTTP API on :WebApp:.
  - `POST /api/livekit/token` accepts a JSON body with `roomName` (string, required, non-empty) and `participantIdentity` (string, required, non-empty).
  - On success returns HTTP 200 with body `{"token": "<jwt>", "url": "<wssUrl>"}` where `token` is a signed LiveKit participant access token and `url` is the LiveKit server WebSocket URL.
  - The token grants the holder permission to join the named room as a publisher and subscriber for audio only (no video). The token expires 10 minutes after issuance.
  - The endpoint reads the LiveKit API key, API secret, and server URL from server-side configuration. These values are not exposed to the browser.
  - POST returns HTTP 400 with body `{"error": "<message>"}` if `roomName` or `participantIdentity` is missing or empty.
  - POST returns HTTP 500 with body `{"error": "livekit not configured"}` if any of the three required configuration values is missing on the server.
- Add the patient call simulator entry page to :WebApp: at the route `GET /simulator`.
  - The page renders a phone-style dial pad with twelve buttons labelled "1".."9", "*", "0", "#", a text display above the dial pad showing the digits the :Patient: has entered, a backspace button to remove the last digit, and a green "Call" button below the dial pad.
  - Tapping a number, "*", or "#" button appends that character to the displayed number; the displayed number is purely cosmetic in this spec and no validation is enforced beyond the next bullet.
  - The "Call" button is disabled while the displayed number has fewer than 7 characters and enabled otherwise.
  - Clicking "Call" does nothing functional in this spec; later specs wire the dial-out flow.
- Wire the simulator "Call" button to start a :Call: and join a LiveKit room.
  - When :Patient: clicks "Call" on `/simulator` and the button is enabled, :WebApp: sends `POST /api/calls` with body `{"patientPhone": "<the displayed number>"}` and reads the returned :Call: `id`.
  - :WebApp: then sends `POST /api/livekit/token` with body `{"roomName": "call-<:Call: id>", "participantIdentity": "patient-<:Call: id>"}` and reads the returned `token` and `url`.
  - :WebApp: requests microphone permission from the browser and joins the LiveKit room at `url` with the obtained `token`, publishing the patient's microphone audio track and subscribing to audio tracks from other participants.
  - While these steps are in flight the page shows a "Connecting…" state in place of the dial pad.
  - If any of the three steps fails, the page returns to the dial-pad state and shows a red banner with text "Could not start the call. Please try again." above the dial pad.
- Render the in-call UI on `/simulator` once the LiveKit room has been joined.
  - The page replaces the dial pad with an in-call panel showing the dialled number at the top, a live microphone-level indicator that animates while the :Patient:'s microphone is active, and a red "Hang up" button at the bottom.
  - The in-call panel also shows the elapsed call duration as `MM:SS`, updated every second, starting at `00:00` when the room is joined.
  - The page plays incoming audio from the room through the browser's audio output.
  - The page hides any prior error banner while the in-call UI is shown.
- Wire the simulator "Hang up" button to end the :Call: and reset the page.
  - Clicking "Hang up" leaves the LiveKit room (releasing the microphone and unpublishing the audio track), then sends `POST /api/calls/:id/end` with body `{"summary": "", "appointmentId": null}` for the current :Call: `id`.
  - After the room has been left and the POST has returned (success or failure), the page returns to the dial-pad state with the displayed number cleared and shows a non-blocking "Call ended" confirmation banner above the dial pad for at least 3 seconds.
- Implement the appointments list HTTP API on :WebApp:.
  - `GET /api/appointments` returns HTTP 200 with a JSON array of all :Appointment: rows ordered by `startAt` ascending, with `id` ascending as a tiebreaker.
  - Each element has fields: `id` (string), `serviceId` (string), `serviceName` (string, the joined :Service: `Name`), `staffId` (string), `staffName` (string, the joined :Staff: `Name`), `patientPhone` (string), `patientName` (string), `startAt` (ISO 8601 local datetime "YYYY-MM-DDTHH:MM"), `endAt` (ISO 8601 local datetime "YYYY-MM-DDTHH:MM"), `status` (string), `createdAt` (ISO 8601 timestamp).
- Add the admin appointments page to :WebApp: at the route `GET /admin/appointments`.
  - On page load the page calls `GET /api/appointments` and renders the returned rows as a read-only table with columns Date, Time, Service, Staff, Patient name, Patient phone, Status, in the order the API returned them.
  - Date is formatted as `EEE, MMM d yyyy` (e.g. "Sun, Jan 5 2026"). Time is formatted as the local time range `HH:MM - HH:MM` (e.g. "09:00 - 10:00").
  - Consecutive rows that share the same Date value are grouped under a date header row that spans the full table width.
  - If the API returns an empty array, the page shows the text "No appointments yet." in place of the table.
  - The page has a header bar with a link labelled "Back to admin" pointing to `/admin`.

  ***acceptance tests***
  - When `GET /api/appointments` returns an empty array, the page should show "No appointments yet." and should not render an appointments table.
  - Consecutive appointments on the same date should appear under one shared date header.
- Implement the call log read HTTP API on :WebApp:.
  - `GET /api/calls` returns HTTP 200 with a JSON array of all :Call: rows ordered by `startedAt` descending. Each element has fields `id`, `patientPhone`, `startedAt`, `endedAt` (nullable), `status`, `summary` (nullable), `appointmentId` (nullable), `outcome` (nullable — absent or null while in progress, one of "resolved" | "needs_callback" | "needs_action" once ended). The `transcript` field is omitted from the list response.
  - The endpoint must not read, select, or return a `createdAt` field for :Call: rows.
  - `GET /api/calls/:id` returns HTTP 200 with the full JSON of one :Call: row, including the `transcript` array and `outcome` (nullable). Each transcript element has fields `speaker` ("patient" or "agent"), `text` (string), `at` (ISO 8601 timestamp).
  - GET `/api/calls/:id` returns HTTP 404 with body `{"error": "not found"}` if no :Call: with that `id` exists.
- Add the admin call log page to :WebApp: at the route `GET /admin/calls`.
  - On page load the page calls `GET /api/calls` and renders the returned rows as a read-only table with columns Started, Ended, Phone, Status, Summary, in the order the API returned them.
  - Started and Ended are formatted as `EEE, MMM d yyyy HH:MM` in local time. An empty Ended cell is rendered as "—". Status displays the raw string. Summary cells are truncated visually to a single line, with the full text shown on hover.
  - Each row links to `/admin/calls/:id`.
  - If the API returns an empty array, the page shows the text "No calls yet." in place of the table.
  - The page has a header bar with a link labelled "Back to admin" pointing to `/admin`.

  ***acceptance tests***
  - When `GET /api/calls` returns an empty array, the page should show "No calls yet." and should not render a call-log table.
  - Each rendered call-log row should navigate to `/admin/calls/:id` for that row's :Call: when clicked.
- Add the admin call detail page to :WebApp: at the route `GET /admin/calls/:id`.
  - On page load the page calls `GET /api/calls/:id`. If the response is HTTP 404 the page renders the text "Call not found." and a link back to `/admin/calls`.
  - On success the page shows a header section with the :Call:'s Phone, Started timestamp, Ended timestamp (or "still in progress"), Status, and Summary.
  - Below the header the page renders the transcript as a chronological list of turns. Each turn shows the speaker label ("Patient" or "Agent") in bold followed by the turn text. Turns from the same speaker are visually grouped with a small vertical gap between speakers.
  - If the transcript array is empty the page shows the text "Transcript is empty." in place of the turn list.
  - The page has a header bar with a link labelled "Back to call log" pointing to `/admin/calls`.

  ***acceptance tests***
  - When `GET /api/calls/:id` returns HTTP 404, the page should show "Call not found." and a link back to `/admin/calls`.
  - When the returned transcript is empty, the page should show "Transcript is empty." and should not render transcript turns.
- Add the admin tasks page to :WebApp: at the route `GET /admin/tasks`.
  - On page load the page calls `GET /api/tasks?status=open` and renders the returned rows as a table with columns: Created (formatted `EEE, MMM d yyyy HH:MM`), Patient Name (shows "—" if `patientName` is empty), Patient Phone, Topic (rendered as a small grey badge if non-empty; shows "—" if empty), Type (badge: "Callback" for type "callback", "Action" for type "action"), Priority (badge: "High" styled in amber for priority "high", "Normal" styled in grey for priority "normal"), Description, Call (an icon or text link to `/admin/calls/:callId`), and Actions.
  - The Actions column has a "Mark done" button per open row. Clicking it sends `PUT /api/tasks/:id` with body `{"status": "done"}` and on HTTP 200 removes the row from the table without a full page reload.
  - Above the table the page shows three filter buttons: "Open", "Completed", and "All". The active filter is highlighted. Clicking "Open" fetches `GET /api/tasks?status=open`; clicking "Completed" fetches `GET /api/tasks?status=done`; clicking "All" fetches `GET /api/tasks?status=all`. The default active filter is "Open". In "Completed" and "All" views, done tasks are displayed with a muted/grayed style and a "Done" status badge but no "Mark done" button.
  - If the filtered result is empty, the page shows the text "No open tasks." (when the "Open" filter is active), "No completed tasks yet." (when the "Completed" filter is active), or "No tasks yet." (when the "All" filter is active) in place of the table.

  ***acceptance tests***
  - When `GET /api/tasks?status=open` returns an empty array, the page should show "No open tasks." and should not render a task table.
  - After clicking "Mark done" on a task row that returns HTTP 200, that row should be removed from the table immediately.
- Add a top navigation bar to every admin page in :WebApp:.
  - Every page whose path begins with `/admin` renders a navigation bar at the top of the page containing seven links: "Dashboard" -> `/admin`, "Practice" -> `/admin/practice`, "Services" -> `/admin/services`, "Staff" -> `/admin/staff`, "Appointments" -> `/admin/appointments`, "Call log" -> `/admin/calls`, "Tasks" -> `/admin/tasks`.
  - The link whose target matches the current page (or the closest parent path under `/admin/staff/:id/hours` -> "Staff", `/admin/calls/:id` -> "Call log", and `/admin/tasks` -> "Tasks") is rendered with a visually distinct active style (for example a bold weight and an underline).
  - The navigation bar replaces any prior per-page "Back to admin" or "Back to call log" header link defined by earlier specs.

  ***acceptance tests***
  - On `/admin/staff/example-id/hours`, the "Staff" navigation link should have the active style and no "Back to admin" link should be rendered.
  - On `/admin/calls/example-id`, the "Call log" navigation link should have the active style and no "Back to call log" link should be rendered.
  - On `/admin/tasks`, the "Tasks" navigation link should have the active style.
- Add the admin dashboard page to :WebApp: at the route `GET /admin`.
  - The page renders :AdminDashboard: as the landing page for :PracticeAdmin:.
  - On page load the page calls `GET /api/practice`, `GET /api/services`, `GET /api/staff`, `GET /api/appointments`, `GET /api/calls`, and `GET /api/tasks?status=open`.
  - The page shows a setup section with three status rows: Practice profile, Services, and Staff.
  - The Practice profile row shows "Complete" when `GET /api/practice` returns a non-empty object, and "Missing" when it returns `{}`.
  - The Services row shows "Complete" when the services response contains at least one row, and "Missing" when it is empty.
  - The Staff row shows "Complete" when the staff response contains at least one row, and "Missing" when it is empty.
  - The page shows five summary counters: total services, total staff, upcoming booked appointments, total calls, and open staff tasks.
  - Upcoming booked appointments count only :Appointment: rows whose `status` is "booked" and whose `startAt` is not earlier than the current local date and time.
  - The open staff tasks counter is derived from the length of the `GET /api/tasks?status=open` response. When the count is greater than zero, the counter is displayed with an amber highlight to draw attention.
  - The page shows quick links labelled "Edit practice", "Manage services", "Manage staff", "View appointments", "View call log", and "View tasks" that navigate to `/admin/practice`, `/admin/services`, `/admin/staff`, `/admin/appointments`, `/admin/calls`, and `/admin/tasks`.
  - If any dashboard data request fails, the page still renders the navigation bar and shows the text "Dashboard data could not be loaded." in place of the setup section and summary counters.
- Implement the setup-data part of the demo seed HTTP API on :WebApp:.
  - `POST /api/demo/seed` creates or updates the setup records in :DemoSeedData: and returns HTTP 200 with a JSON summary object.
  - The summary object has top-level keys `practice`, `services`, `staff`, and `workingHours`. Each value has `created` and `updated` integer counts.
  - If no :Practice: row exists, the endpoint creates a demo :Practice: with name `Bright Smile Dental Studio`, address `1250 Maple Avenue, Springfield`, PhoneDisplay `(555) 014-2378`, GreetingStyle `warm`, ToneProfile `friendly`, ClosingStyle `standard`, AgentGender `female`, AgentName `Maya`, and HoursOfOperation Monday through Friday from `09:00` to `17:00`.
  - If a :Practice: row already exists, the endpoint preserves it and reports zero practice records created and zero practice records updated.
  - The endpoint creates or updates three demo :Service: rows, each with a non-empty English description, category, and pre/post procedure instructions:
    - `Routine Cleaning`: DurationMinutes 60, PriceCents 12000, Category "Preventive", PreProcedureInstructions "Please brush and floss before your appointment.", PostProcedureInstructions "Avoid eating or drinking for 30 minutes after your cleaning."
    - `Dental Checkup`: DurationMinutes 30, PriceCents 8500, Category "Preventive", PreProcedureInstructions "No special preparation is needed for your checkup.", PostProcedureInstructions "You can eat and drink normally after your checkup."
    - `Teeth Whitening`: DurationMinutes 90, PriceCents 25000, Category "Cosmetic", PreProcedureInstructions "Avoid coffee, tea, red wine, and dark foods for 24 hours before your whitening session.", PostProcedureInstructions "Avoid staining foods and drinks for 24 hours after your whitening treatment."
  - The endpoint creates or updates three demo :Staff: rows, each with a short non-empty English bio, qualifications, and specialization: `Dr. Maya Chen` with Role `Dentist`, Qualifications `DMD`, Specialization `Cosmetic Dentistry`; `Dr. Oliver Grant` with Role `Dentist`, Qualifications `DDS`, Specialization `General Dentistry`; and `Sofia Ramirez` with Role `Hygienist`, Qualifications `RDH, BS`, Specialization `Preventive Care`.
  - The endpoint creates or updates Monday through Friday demo :WorkingHours: rows for each demo :Staff: member. Demo dentists work `09:00` to `17:00`; the demo hygienist works `08:30` to `15:30`.
  - The endpoint never deletes rows and never changes non-demo :Service:, :Staff:, or :WorkingHours: rows.
  - Running the endpoint more than once reuses the same demo-owned setup records instead of creating duplicates.
- Extend `POST /api/demo/seed` to create demo appointments and calls.
  - The endpoint creates or updates three demo :Appointment: rows with Status `booked`, distributed across the seeded services and staff.
  - Demo appointment start times are future local datetimes relative to the seed run so the dashboard always has upcoming booked appointments after seeding.
  - The endpoint creates or updates three ended demo :Call: rows.
  - Each demo :Call: has a non-empty PatientPhone, StartedAt, EndedAt, Status `ended`, Summary, and at least two transcript turns.
  - At least one demo :Call: references one seeded :Appointment: through AppointmentRef.
  - The response summary object also has top-level keys `appointments` and `calls`, each with `created` and `updated` integer counts.
  - Running the endpoint more than once reuses the same demo-owned appointments and calls instead of creating duplicates.
  - The endpoint never deletes rows and never changes non-demo :Appointment: or :Call: rows.
- Implement the demo metadata HTTP API on :WebApp:.
  - `GET /api/demo/metadata` returns HTTP 200 with JSON body `{"services": ["<id>"], "staff": ["<id>"], "workingHours": ["<id>"], "appointments": ["<id>"], "calls": ["<id>"]}`.
  - Each array contains the ids of records currently marked as demo-owned for that entity type.
  - If no demo-owned records exist for an entity type, the corresponding array is empty.
  - The endpoint does not include the :Practice: row because the single practice row may be user-owned even after seeding.
  - Demo ownership metadata is internal to :WebApp: and is omitted from `GET /api/practice`, `GET /api/services`, `GET /api/staff`, `GET /api/staff/:staffId/working-hours`, `GET /api/appointments`, `GET /api/calls`, and `GET /api/calls/:id`.
- Add demo seeding controls to :AdminDashboard:.
  - The `/admin` page shows a button labelled "Seed demo data".
  - Clicking "Seed demo data" opens a confirmation prompt before any data is changed.
  - If :PracticeAdmin: cancels the prompt, no HTTP request is sent and the page stays unchanged.
  - If :PracticeAdmin: confirms the prompt, the page sends `POST /api/demo/seed`.
  - While the request is in flight, the seed button is disabled.
  - On HTTP 200, the page refreshes the same dashboard data it loads on page load and shows a non-blocking success message that includes the summary counts returned by the endpoint.
  - On any non-200 response or network error, the page shows a non-blocking error banner with text `Demo data could not be seeded.` and leaves the previously loaded dashboard data visible.
- Show demo badges on admin pages.
  - The services, staff, staff working-hours, appointments, call log, and call detail admin pages call `GET /api/demo/metadata` when they load.
  - On the services page, each :Service: row whose id appears in `services` shows a small badge with text `Demo`.
  - On the staff page, each :Staff: row whose id appears in `staff` shows a small badge with text `Demo`.
  - On the staff working-hours page, each :WorkingHours: row whose id appears in `workingHours` shows a small badge with text `Demo`.
  - On the appointments page, each :Appointment: row whose id appears in `appointments` shows a small badge with text `Demo`.
  - On the call log page, each :Call: row whose id appears in `calls` shows a small badge with text `Demo`.
  - On the call detail page, the header section shows a small badge with text `Demo` when that :Call:'s id appears in `calls`.
  - If `GET /api/demo/metadata` fails, the admin page still renders its main data and omits demo badges.
